WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.311 --> 00:00:03.173
March 2023,

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:07.234
the world's largest Egyptology museum is set to open at the foot of the Giza Pyramid.

00:00:08.134 --> 00:00:08.853
For the occasion,

00:00:09.153 --> 00:00:13.214
the finest treasures of ancient Egypt are being transferred to this colossal new building,

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:15.155
which cost over a billion euros.

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:16.595
For the first time,

00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:21.977
this museum will bring together the 5,000 sumptuous pieces from the legendary tomb of Tutankhamun,

00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:23.617
15 kilometers from here,

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:25.598
in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo,

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:29.799
in the former exhibition halls for the jewels of the most famous of pharaohs.

00:00:30.292 --> 00:00:35.456
Another treasure discovered during one of the most fabulous adventures in archaeology takes its place.

00:00:36.615 --> 00:00:38.737
Everyone knows about Tutankhamen's tomb,

00:00:39.237 --> 00:00:44.942
but few people know that in fact we have several other royal tombs that were found intact,

00:00:45.382 --> 00:00:47.764
and these are the treasures of Tanis.

00:00:48.103 --> 00:00:52.746
This is something that is really marvelous because it's so unknown,

00:00:53.268 --> 00:00:56.550
but probably more spectacular than Tutankhamen in a certain way.

00:00:57.651 --> 00:00:59.672
The treasures of Tanis are exceptional.

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:02.040
Over 60 kilos of gold,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.420
two one-of-a-kind silver coffins,

00:01:05.341 --> 00:01:10.623
funeral masks without equal—they were found in a forgotten necropolis in the Nile Delta,

00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:13.263
a true valley of kings in northern Egypt.

00:01:14.885 --> 00:01:20.706
It reveals a little-known side of ancient Egypt and the saga of long-forgotten pharaohs who,

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.406
like Tutankhamun before them,

00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:25.048
left their mark on Egyptian civilization.

00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:27.873
People don't know the names of

00:01:28.253 --> 00:01:31.896
Susenes or Asorkon or Takht or people like that.

00:01:32.357 --> 00:01:35.019
Yet these kings ruled Egypt,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.221
and most excitingly,

00:01:37.842 --> 00:01:39.824
their tombs were found intact,

00:01:40.224 --> 00:01:41.786
filled with coffins,

00:01:42.126 --> 00:01:42.967
sarcophagi,

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:44.009
and jewels.

00:01:44.948 --> 00:01:45.549
There is gold.

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:46.330
There is silver.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.291
There is treasures,

00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:48.992
and there's a great story behind that.

00:01:50.173 --> 00:01:51.735
Nothing like them had ever been found.

00:01:51.996 --> 00:01:56.218
But it never really captured the public imagination in the way that Tanegami did.

00:01:57.018 --> 00:01:58.639
The reason bad timing.

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:04.881
The discovery of the royal necropolis at Tanis coincided with the outbreak of the Second World War.

00:02:05.582 --> 00:02:08.823
So people are paying attention to what happens in Europe,

00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:11.364
to the tensions between the countries,

00:02:11.843 --> 00:02:19.687
and this is so important that ancient Egypt kind of suddenly becomes more and more unimportant for the public.

00:02:20.087 --> 00:02:21.307
So it doesn't make.

00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:22.268
The news media,

00:02:22.427 --> 00:02:22.867
the way

00:02:23.588 --> 00:02:24.508
Tarenkamen did,

00:02:24.548 --> 00:02:25.928
or the way you would think it would.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:32.450
Yet it is one of the most fascinating and extraordinary episodes in the history of Egyptology.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.191
And it's the work of a Frenchman,

00:02:35.551 --> 00:02:36.351
Pierre Montet,

00:02:37.231 --> 00:02:39.052
though his name may not be well known.

00:02:39.452 --> 00:02:40.372
Tanis is,

00:02:40.552 --> 00:02:42.093
thanks to a legendary film.

00:02:43.693 --> 00:02:48.536
Tanis is a site that is not just known for its treasures,

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.815
but it's also known.

00:02:50.115 --> 00:02:50.975
From something else,

00:02:50.995 --> 00:02:53.316
it's known from a famous Hollywood movie.

00:02:55.277 --> 00:03:03.519
Tanis is where the climatic scenes of *Raiders of the Lost Ark* movie are to be found.

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:05.300
As is evident in this scene,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.102
the archaeological site of Tanis is at the heart of the plot of Steven Spielberg's film.

00:03:10.542 --> 00:03:13.503
It's the place where Indiana Jones takes place.

00:03:20.528 --> 00:03:25.091
This masterpiece of adventure cinema is loosely based on the real history of the site,

00:03:25.412 --> 00:03:30.355
a city revived by an Egyptologist and his team against the backdrop of a world in chaos.

00:03:30.875 --> 00:03:33.057
To understand the significance of this discovery,

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:35.778
you must travel a thousand kilometers from Tanis.

00:03:49.036 --> 00:03:50.116
In 1921,

00:03:50.417 --> 00:03:53.438
Pierre Montet began excavations at Byblos in Lebanon.

00:03:54.038 --> 00:03:54.599
At the time,

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:55.099
he was a

00:03:55.639 --> 00:03:58.842
36-year-old archaeologist and professor at the University of Strasbourg.

00:03:59.262 --> 00:04:00.022
In three years,

00:04:00.182 --> 00:04:03.905
he unearthed the oldest Phoenician inscription ever identified and exhumed.

00:04:03.985 --> 00:04:05.446
The tombs of the city's princes,

00:04:05.746 --> 00:04:07.147
some of which are still intact.

00:04:07.707 --> 00:04:09.027
When he opened the sarcophagi,

00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:10.948
he made an astonishing discovery.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:15.731
The rulers there regarded themselves very much as the friends of the Egyptians,

00:04:16.031 --> 00:04:19.192
and they were buried with treasures which included

00:04:19.774 --> 00:04:20.913
Egyptian items.

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:26.536
The remains are covered in Egyptian treasures that are as wondrous as they are beautiful.

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:32.178
This find confirms the close links between Egypt and Phoenicia in antiquity.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:37.062
Pierre Monte found what he was looking for in the years to come,

00:04:37.841 --> 00:04:40.442
and he wanted to trace when it started,

00:04:40.603 --> 00:04:41.463
its extent,

00:04:41.824 --> 00:04:46.386
and what it meant—not just for the history of Lebanon or the history of Egypt,

00:04:46.745 --> 00:04:49.086
but for the entire Mediterranean world.

00:04:49.266 --> 00:04:54.249
He's very much interested in this connection between between the cultures.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:56.811
He's finding lots of evidence of Egypt in Lebanon,

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.351
and he's perhaps beginning to think what.

00:04:59.892 --> 00:05:03.031
Evidence might there be of foreign cultures back in Egypt?

00:05:03.816 --> 00:05:08.299
It was with this goal in mind that he found himself on the delta of the Nile in Egypt.

00:05:09.079 --> 00:05:11.240
One site in particular drew his attention:

00:05:11.881 --> 00:05:12.302
Tanis.

00:05:12.922 --> 00:05:15.364
It is a site known for a very long time,

00:05:16.064 --> 00:05:17.985
that has a very long history,

00:05:18.626 --> 00:05:19.867
a very important site.

00:05:19.907 --> 00:05:23.588
Just judging from the monuments that are lying around everywhere on the surface,

00:05:23.788 --> 00:05:28.872
the city had been excavated to some extent by archaeologists previously,

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:32.235
but they had by no means finished the job.

00:05:32.335 --> 00:05:33.576
There was a lot more.

00:05:33.615 --> 00:05:34.656
Still there to discover.

00:05:35.517 --> 00:05:39.640
Could Tanis bear any traces of the Phoenicians or other Near Eastern people?

00:05:40.382 --> 00:05:42.442
Pierre Monte is convinced that it does.

00:05:43.764 --> 00:05:46.165
From 1929 to 1939,

00:05:46.466 --> 00:05:48.788
Pierre Monte multiplies his discoveries.

00:05:49.568 --> 00:05:51.129
In 10 years of excavations,

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:56.113
he identifies most of the major monuments in this multi-layered archaeological site.

00:05:56.615 --> 00:06:00.697
Monte makes the city start to rise from the ashes.

00:06:01.177 --> 00:06:02.778
It's now beginning to look like a real.

00:06:03.939 --> 00:06:04.560
Ruined city,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.624
rather than just simply a disaster zone.

00:06:09.264 --> 00:06:17.831
The layout of all of this were a huge boost to our understanding of an urban and religious center in the delta,

00:06:18.492 --> 00:06:24.516
as well as bringing Tanis from obscurity into the forefront of the Egyptological world.

00:06:24.877 --> 00:06:26.939
The most important is the Temple of Amun.

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:28.399
This colossal structure,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.762
measuring four hundred by three hundred meters,

00:06:31.041 --> 00:06:32.723
is the size of the Louvre Museum.

00:06:32.903 --> 00:06:33.183
It is a

00:06:33.283 --> 00:06:34.965
Massive temple enclosure,

00:06:35.485 --> 00:06:40.590
befitting the kind of religious site devoted to the Egyptians'

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:41.691
principal deity,

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:42.371
Amun.

00:06:43.412 --> 00:06:46.514
But no one was expecting the arrival of new enemies.

00:06:46.814 --> 00:06:52.359
The Nazis have been increasing pressure in Europe since seizing power in Berlin in 1933.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.381
The whole world is marching to war.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.603
The Nazis are in power in Germany.

00:06:58.244 --> 00:07:02.508
They are beginning to build a very strong nation.

00:07:02.828 --> 00:07:03.067
Strong.

00:07:03.308 --> 00:07:04.669
Economically strong,

00:07:04.929 --> 00:07:05.570
militarily,

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.531
the Egyptologist is increasingly worried.

00:07:08.752 --> 00:07:11.193
He fears he will not be able to complete his work.

00:07:12.574 --> 00:07:14.415
If hostilities actually break out,

00:07:14.895 --> 00:07:15.236
how?

00:07:16.076 --> 00:07:17.456
Can work be carried on?

00:07:18.497 --> 00:07:20.939
What could happen if work is suspended?

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:25.201
Why the political situation in nineteen thirty-nine is so problematical?

00:07:26.081 --> 00:07:28.263
Pierre Montet sets out to go to Tanis,

00:07:28.663 --> 00:07:30.724
and he will make the discovery of his life.

00:07:31.024 --> 00:07:36.247
It will be the probably most important season of excavation of Montet,

00:07:36.727 --> 00:07:43.331
and it's one of the most important seasons of an excavation in all the history of Egyptian archaeology.

00:07:44.331 --> 00:07:45.872
January nineteen thirty-nine.

00:07:46.432 --> 00:07:48.314
Despite fears of a second world war,

00:07:48.674 --> 00:07:51.757
Pierre Montet begins his eleventh season of excavations.

00:07:52.197 --> 00:07:55.139
When he goes to Egypt for that season,

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:02.425
Montet is unaware that that season will bring to light something unimaginable,

00:08:02.985 --> 00:08:09.730
something that makes whatever he'd found over the previous ten years completely fade into the background.

00:08:10.292 --> 00:08:14.435
The Egyptologist and his team move tons of earth in search of new clues,

00:08:15.035 --> 00:08:15.696
and then comes.

00:08:15.596 --> 00:08:20.580
Comes the startling realization that if powerful pharaohs have succeeded one another here,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.282
is it possible they are also buried here?

00:08:23.843 --> 00:08:25.124
Where were the people buried?

00:08:25.364 --> 00:08:26.505
Where are the cemeteries?

00:08:27.365 --> 00:08:28.406
In the surrounding area,

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:30.008
no tombs are discovered,

00:08:30.428 --> 00:08:31.249
no necropolis,

00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:33.450
and even less a royal necropolis.

00:08:34.171 --> 00:08:35.552
If this is northern Thebes,

00:08:35.792 --> 00:08:40.076
then logically there must be the equivalent of the Valley of the Kings.

00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:43.759
The Valley of the Kings near Thebes,

00:08:43.899 --> 00:08:45.120
in present-day Luxor.

00:08:45.479 --> 00:08:47.441
Was home to the tombs of the pharaohs of the

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:48.781
18th, 19th,

00:08:48.783 --> 00:08:50.062
and 20th dynasties,

00:08:50.461 --> 00:08:51.622
those of Tutankhamen,

00:08:51.942 --> 00:08:52.462
Ramses,

00:08:52.622 --> 00:08:53.283
and Thutmose.

00:08:54.063 --> 00:08:58.165
The final resting place of these illustrious monarchs was outside the city.

00:09:01.086 --> 00:09:02.508
We have the Valley of the Kings.

00:09:02.567 --> 00:09:06.309
We have all these famous tombs of the great pharaohs of that period.

00:09:06.909 --> 00:09:07.990
But after this period,

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.871
the tombs of the kings are missing.

00:09:10.679 --> 00:09:11.740
The reason is simple.

00:09:12.161 --> 00:09:13.062
All these tombs,

00:09:13.201 --> 00:09:14.802
with the exception of Tutankhamun's,

00:09:15.123 --> 00:09:16.683
were plundered in ancient times,

00:09:17.004 --> 00:09:19.625
leading subsequent pharaohs to change their strategy,

00:09:20.025 --> 00:09:21.486
particularly in Tanis.

00:09:21.947 --> 00:09:25.529
What they decide is that instead of burying the pharaohs.

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:27.573
Out in the secret desert,

00:09:28.193 --> 00:09:31.277
they'll put them where everybody can see them—in the middle of the temple.

00:09:31.838 --> 00:09:33.560
We are inside a sacred area.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.765
Never before have kings considered building tombs inside a sacred area.

00:09:38.205 --> 00:09:40.348
This is a domain reserved for the gods.

00:09:41.509 --> 00:09:44.951
Pierre Monte continues his excavations around the Temple of Ammon.

00:09:45.591 --> 00:09:48.093
He explores a number of mud brick buildings,

00:09:48.413 --> 00:09:49.274
and at one point,

00:09:49.594 --> 00:09:51.776
something fairly intriguing comes to light.

00:09:52.176 --> 00:09:53.277
At the foot of a building,

00:09:53.397 --> 00:09:55.478
a circular pit appears that has been filled.

00:09:55.538 --> 00:09:55.678
Then,

00:09:56.138 --> 00:09:57.119
the workers clear it,

00:09:57.339 --> 00:09:58.440
and at the bottom of the pit,

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:01.162
they discover a stone slab—a very,

00:10:01.242 --> 00:10:02.763
very large piece of stone,

00:10:02.783 --> 00:10:03.283
which he thought,

00:10:03.363 --> 00:10:03.763
"Great,

00:10:04.323 --> 00:10:05.324
this is the ground,"

00:10:06.125 --> 00:10:06.865
except it wasn't.

00:10:08.126 --> 00:10:09.006
But what could it be?

00:10:10.727 --> 00:10:14.810
Pierre Montet quickly had the eight meters of earth above the slab excavated.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.251
He falls upon a flat surface,

00:10:17.311 --> 00:10:18.392
several meters long.

00:10:20.253 --> 00:10:23.715
The slab seems to continue until a protruding surface appears.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:25.962
While clearing it,

00:10:26.203 --> 00:10:30.997
he discovers several fragments of what are known as Ushabti funerary statuettes.

00:10:32.216 --> 00:10:33.456
Funerary material,

00:10:33.617 --> 00:10:35.338
material that would accompany a burial.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:35.458
Or,

00:10:36.299 --> 00:10:37.300
generally speaking,

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:38.761
when you find a chabti,

00:10:39.162 --> 00:10:41.203
you're potentially in a necropolis area.

00:10:42.985 --> 00:10:44.206
There's no room for doubt.

00:10:44.766 --> 00:10:47.829
Pierre Monte has just made an unprecedented discovery.

00:10:48.591 --> 00:10:50.172
If these statuettes are there,

00:10:50.611 --> 00:10:52.933
it's because the sarcophagus of the deceased,

00:10:53.314 --> 00:10:53.995
his treasures,

00:10:54.115 --> 00:10:56.076
and his mummy are in a nearby tomb.

00:11:00.172 --> 00:11:03.874
He carefully inspects the relics and discovers the name of the dead man,

00:11:04.335 --> 00:11:05.735
and it's not just anyone.

00:11:06.796 --> 00:11:08.317
This is Shekhkhun the Third,

00:11:08.697 --> 00:11:09.457
a pharaoh of the

00:11:10.018 --> 00:11:13.600
22nd Dynasty who reigned 400 years after Ramses the Second.

00:11:14.841 --> 00:11:18.403
Pierre Monte learns from ancient texts that it was the capital of the

00:11:18.984 --> 00:11:20.524
21st and 22nd Dynasties.

00:11:21.245 --> 00:11:25.307
These two dynasties represent one of the darkest periods in Egyptian history.

00:11:31.347 --> 00:11:32.589
Around 1000 B.C.,

00:11:33.129 --> 00:11:37.615
the unity of the empire was shaken after the death of Pharaoh Ramses the Eleventh.

00:11:38.576 --> 00:11:39.756
Ramesses the Eleventh,

00:11:39.776 --> 00:11:41.976
who was the last king of the New Kingdom,

00:11:42.016 --> 00:11:43.997
the last of the Ramesside kings,

00:11:45.119 --> 00:11:46.639
had no sons to succeed him.

00:11:47.159 --> 00:11:48.659
He only had two daughters,

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.800
and so there were two rivals for power at the time:

00:11:51.821 --> 00:11:53.641
the priests of Amen in Thebes,

00:11:53.643 --> 00:11:54.962
who were getting very powerful,

00:11:55.263 --> 00:11:56.622
and then the kings in the Delta.

00:11:57.303 --> 00:12:04.446
So the the country was almost split because we had the high priests of Amen then declaring themselves king in Thebes,

00:12:04.846 --> 00:12:08.367
but also the rulers then in the Delta also declaring themselves.

00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:12.594
These kings of the Delta were the pharaohs of the

00:12:13.155 --> 00:12:14.637
21st and 22nd dynasties.

00:12:15.398 --> 00:12:17.340
They ruled northern Egypt between the mid

00:12:17.701 --> 00:12:19.363
11th and late 8th centuries BC.

00:12:21.163 --> 00:12:22.804
Those of the twenty-second dynasty,

00:12:23.084 --> 00:12:24.384
including Tchitchonk III,

00:12:24.884 --> 00:12:30.086
came from one of the tribes of distant Libyan origin who had settled long ago in the Egyptian Delta.

00:12:30.626 --> 00:12:33.346
Very little was known where they had lived,

00:12:33.526 --> 00:12:35.707
where their city was had been located.

00:12:35.767 --> 00:12:38.608
Their capital city was not even clearly established.

00:12:38.628 --> 00:12:41.809
We just have every now and then a mention of them in some texts,

00:12:42.329 --> 00:12:44.029
a very few small monuments.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.891
We cannot really write a history of this period at that point.

00:12:49.311 --> 00:12:49.431
So.

00:12:49.851 --> 00:12:51.052
This was a bit of a mystery.

00:12:51.774 --> 00:12:53.235
He continues his inspection,

00:12:53.595 --> 00:12:55.817
and in one place spots a slight anomaly.

00:12:56.798 --> 00:13:00.441
He finds a gap between what appears to be the stones of this floor,

00:13:01.341 --> 00:13:05.205
and puts a stick down and finds there is a cavity below.

00:13:05.805 --> 00:13:07.747
It's not a floor; it's a ceiling.

00:13:08.086 --> 00:13:08.747
Beneath it,

00:13:09.508 --> 00:13:10.609
there is a void,

00:13:10.969 --> 00:13:13.271
and that void is filled with,

00:13:13.391 --> 00:13:14.372
perhaps one would say,

00:13:14.471 --> 00:13:15.493
wonderful things.

00:13:15.932 --> 00:13:19.076
He must have been fantastically excited.

00:13:19.355 --> 00:13:21.278
He must have known there was the possibility,

00:13:21.498 --> 00:13:23.480
even if it was just a tiny chance,

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:26.242
that he was going to find something amazing,

00:13:26.302 --> 00:13:27.883
something to rival Tutankhamun.

00:13:28.724 --> 00:13:29.685
It's quite exceptional.

00:13:30.025 --> 00:13:31.847
We are on the trail of a large building,

00:13:32.127 --> 00:13:33.368
perhaps the necropolis.

00:13:34.569 --> 00:13:36.991
Pierre Monte immediately had the slab cleared,

00:13:37.251 --> 00:13:39.133
but disappointment is the order of the day.

00:13:39.353 --> 00:13:44.717
He realizes that it masks an old thief's hole filled in with two small limestone blocks.

00:13:44.878 --> 00:13:47.600
This hole is every archaeologist's nightmare.

00:13:47.936 --> 00:13:52.017
It means that somebody has got there before.

00:13:52.116 --> 00:13:55.298
So the joy of discovering and entering a tomb is immense,

00:13:55.538 --> 00:13:56.418
but at the same time,

00:13:56.638 --> 00:14:00.339
there's great apprehension and a near certainty that the tomb has been looted.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:02.480
In ancient times,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:05.500
tombs were ravaged by thieves in search of gold.

00:14:06.281 --> 00:14:08.642
The Valley of the Kings was completely looted,

00:14:09.022 --> 00:14:10.981
with the exception of Tutankhamen's tomb.

00:14:11.701 --> 00:14:14.082
Could this burial site have escaped the brigands?

00:14:15.644 --> 00:14:17.163
So he starts opening this hole.

00:14:18.764 --> 00:14:25.347
Pierre Monte has the two limestone blocks removed and contemplates the interior of a two-meter-high room.

00:14:27.848 --> 00:14:28.969
February 27,

00:14:28.971 --> 00:14:30.591
1939.

00:14:30.591 --> 00:14:33.572
The Egyptologist and his team enter this small room.

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:35.733
It's otherwise very dark inside.

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:37.714
They were able to see,

00:14:39.055 --> 00:14:39.555
more or less,

00:14:40.235 --> 00:14:41.437
equipped with a flashlight.

00:14:41.756 --> 00:14:46.139
Pierre Monte illuminates the walls of the room and notices that they are decorated.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:50.901
Hieroglyphics and engravings gradually revealed themselves before his eyes,

00:14:51.661 --> 00:14:52.282
but above all,

00:14:52.622 --> 00:14:54.382
he faces a royal cartouche,

00:14:54.782 --> 00:14:56.363
the signature of the pharaohs.

00:14:57.483 --> 00:15:01.084
So he knows that he is dealing not with the tomb of someone,

00:15:01.364 --> 00:15:02.464
but with the tomb of a king.

00:15:03.264 --> 00:15:07.826
Pierre Monte manages to read the name of the owner of the tomb he has just entered.

00:15:08.245 --> 00:15:09.906
It is King Osorkon II,

00:15:10.446 --> 00:15:12.867
a well-known king of the 22nd dynasty.

00:15:13.287 --> 00:15:15.788
After Shepseskhep III.

00:15:16.275 --> 00:15:18.816
Here is a second pharaoh of the 22nd dynasty.

00:15:19.316 --> 00:15:20.957
Pierre Monte doesn't know it yet,

00:15:21.317 --> 00:15:24.439
but he's just gotten his hands on a royal Egyptian necropolis,

00:15:24.779 --> 00:15:26.960
a kind of Valley of the Kings of the North.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.322
But who is this Osorkon the Second?

00:15:29.901 --> 00:15:30.581
For Monte,

00:15:32.002 --> 00:15:34.123
Osorkon the Second is a name he knows.

00:15:35.323 --> 00:15:39.145
He is one of the more important kings of his period,

00:15:40.146 --> 00:15:43.307
and various objects naming him have been found.

00:15:43.608 --> 00:15:44.309
Over the years,

00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:46.950
so his place in history is reasonably secure.

00:15:47.151 --> 00:15:48.431
We know he's quite important,

00:15:48.451 --> 00:15:50.593
but we don't know much else about him.

00:15:51.333 --> 00:15:52.554
Could this be his tomb?

00:15:55.356 --> 00:15:55.676
If so,

00:15:56.157 --> 00:15:57.938
what might it reveal about this pharaoh?

00:16:00.039 --> 00:16:08.685
The discovery of his sarcophagus and treasure would reveal a great deal more about him and one of ancient Egypt's least known dynasties.

00:16:15.328 --> 00:16:16.369
Then not only is it,

00:16:16.668 --> 00:16:16.908
you know,

00:16:16.909 --> 00:16:23.370
a sensational discovery in terms of finding the place where a king is buried and perhaps finding some burial equipment.

00:16:24.211 --> 00:16:27.471
We know that the tomb of Tutankhamun is full of treasure.

00:16:29.011 --> 00:16:30.373
But on top of all of that,

00:16:30.712 --> 00:16:36.093
there's the possibility here to enormously extend what we know about this period of history.

00:16:37.153 --> 00:16:38.554
Excitement is at its peak.

00:16:39.355 --> 00:16:40.995
At the far end of the first room,

00:16:41.134 --> 00:16:42.775
entered by Montet and his team.

00:16:43.156 --> 00:16:45.317
An opening leads to a second chamber.

00:16:46.518 --> 00:16:48.278
Probably the entrance to something,

00:16:48.518 --> 00:16:51.160
a passage into this kind of building.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:54.541
At the far end is a square opening,

00:16:54.781 --> 00:16:56.602
also about a meter by a meter,

00:16:56.782 --> 00:16:59.203
which leads visibly into a second room.

00:16:59.243 --> 00:16:59.883
He enters.

00:17:00.003 --> 00:17:00.123
So

00:17:00.624 --> 00:17:02.985
Monte goes through with this flickering light,

00:17:03.065 --> 00:17:03.605
looking in,

00:17:03.805 --> 00:17:08.027
and there he finds a burial chamber with what looks like a burial.

00:17:09.106 --> 00:17:10.228
A burial chamber.

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:13.020
Every Egyptologist dreams.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:20.903
Pierre Monte is the first to contemplate what no one has seen for millennia—the interior of this pharaoh's tomb.

00:17:21.985 --> 00:17:22.765
Unfortunately,

00:17:23.125 --> 00:17:24.706
it has been emptied of its treasure.

00:17:26.026 --> 00:17:27.807
Looking round on the walls,

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:32.629
they realize these are inscriptions of King Aazorkon II.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:37.351
These are scenes of him in the underworld,

00:17:38.171 --> 00:17:39.572
scenes from the Book of the Dead.

00:17:40.228 --> 00:17:43.671
A scene of his heart being weighed in front of Osiris.

00:17:44.492 --> 00:17:47.815
In the center of the room is a pink granite sarcophagus.

00:17:49.737 --> 00:17:54.581
What they have found is absolutely spellbinding,

00:17:55.883 --> 00:18:01.748
because nobody has ever found a tomb of a king of this period.

00:18:02.829 --> 00:18:05.992
Piermonte immediately sends a telegram to his wife.

00:18:06.624 --> 00:18:08.105
He inspects the sarcophagus,

00:18:08.405 --> 00:18:11.148
but there is no inscription mentioning Osorkon II.

00:18:11.909 --> 00:18:12.870
Is it really his?

00:18:13.769 --> 00:18:14.310
What's more,

00:18:14.611 --> 00:18:16.773
a hole appears in the sarcophagus chamber,

00:18:17.153 --> 00:18:18.413
which doesn't bode well.

00:18:21.197 --> 00:18:22.017
Six days later,

00:18:22.257 --> 00:18:23.097
on March 3rd,

00:18:23.458 --> 00:18:26.961
the Egyptologist and his team are able to open the sarcophagus.

00:18:27.662 --> 00:18:28.241
Of course,

00:18:28.603 --> 00:18:29.864
having seen the hole.

00:18:30.951 --> 00:18:33.253
Monte is slightly prepared for the worst,

00:18:33.333 --> 00:18:36.235
and when he opens the sarcophagus and clears it over,

00:18:36.536 --> 00:18:37.957
he sees that it is empty.

00:18:39.318 --> 00:18:40.079
Disappointingly,

00:18:40.219 --> 00:18:41.960
the coffin has also been looted.

00:18:45.983 --> 00:18:51.388
Inside is nothing more than a few bones and some fragments of gilded wood.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:53.990
So,

00:18:54.070 --> 00:19:00.135
no sign of the treasures that one would expect from tomb of a pharaoh.

00:19:00.524 --> 00:19:02.625
Monte is clearly disappointed,

00:19:03.766 --> 00:19:06.568
but there may be more to be found in the tomb.

00:19:07.088 --> 00:19:08.329
Despite the disappointment,

00:19:08.489 --> 00:19:11.991
the discovery of this tomb is a great step forward for Egyptology.

00:19:12.331 --> 00:19:14.292
But the archaeologist doesn't stop there.

00:19:14.572 --> 00:19:21.336
He goes on searching for more because he starts realizing probably there's more than just this one tomb.

00:19:22.717 --> 00:19:28.160
Pierre Monte has no idea that he's about to uncover a real necropolis.

00:19:28.631 --> 00:19:34.774
That will help us to understand a whole part of Egyptian history that was unknown to us until then.

00:19:40.576 --> 00:19:47.399
The Egyptologist examines the walls of the sarcophagus chamber and notices that a block is missing from the top of one of them.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:52.622
It appears to be a fairly thin partition with nothing behind it.

00:19:53.162 --> 00:19:54.983
He points out that in fact.

00:19:55.775 --> 00:19:57.456
The proportions are such,

00:19:57.636 --> 00:19:58.756
and the walls are such,

00:19:59.216 --> 00:20:01.317
that there must be another room.

00:20:02.057 --> 00:20:05.718
He crosses to the other side and finds himself in a tiny room,

00:20:06.178 --> 00:20:07.658
still beautifully decorated.

00:20:08.079 --> 00:20:12.961
It features two passages blocked by huge boulders that are impossible to remove.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.761
Could one of them be the real entrance to the tomb,

00:20:16.162 --> 00:20:18.561
or would they lead to other rooms or vaults?

00:20:18.942 --> 00:20:22.364
The boulders that conceal the grave entrances are so heavy,

00:20:22.763 --> 00:20:23.703
so monumental.

00:20:24.043 --> 00:20:25.825
That they have to be maneuvered with hoists,

00:20:26.065 --> 00:20:26.424
pulleys,

00:20:26.644 --> 00:20:27.244
things like that,

00:20:27.566 --> 00:20:28.645
and there wasn't enough room.

00:20:29.906 --> 00:20:33.227
Pierre Monte then decides to approach it from the exterior.

00:20:34.908 --> 00:20:37.529
They realize that it's not just a vault they've entered,

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:41.991
but a whole series of rooms and probably several burials and graves.

00:20:43.111 --> 00:20:45.212
Maybe this is another tomb,

00:20:45.612 --> 00:20:47.614
and maybe it has escaped the robbers.

00:20:48.134 --> 00:20:48.454
And so,

00:20:48.534 --> 00:20:49.654
with great trepidation,

00:20:50.054 --> 00:20:50.836
they enter it.

00:20:52.255 --> 00:20:53.416
It is now March 6,

00:20:53.417 --> 00:20:54.798
1939.

00:20:55.417 --> 00:20:59.900
The archaeologist enters the first small rectangular chamber covered with mud.

00:21:02.562 --> 00:21:05.345
Pierre Montet has the layers of earth removed.

00:21:06.205 --> 00:21:06.625
A new,

00:21:07.046 --> 00:21:09.827
deeply recessed sarcophagus is revealed,

00:21:10.348 --> 00:21:11.470
buried in the ground.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.412
Excitement and joy are,

00:21:14.432 --> 00:21:15.011
of course,

00:21:15.111 --> 00:21:15.712
at their peak.

00:21:17.094 --> 00:21:20.036
A new royal tomb has just been discovered.

00:21:22.167 --> 00:21:27.711
Hundreds of funerary statuettes representing the pharaoh's servants in the afterlife are stored here.

00:21:28.432 --> 00:21:30.613
They reveal the identity of its occupant.

00:21:31.693 --> 00:21:32.994
We're dealing with a new king,

00:21:33.494 --> 00:21:34.316
King Takelot.

00:21:34.775 --> 00:21:35.675
A King Takelot,

00:21:36.156 --> 00:21:39.438
once more a pharaoh of whom we almost know nothing.

00:21:41.579 --> 00:21:44.162
Takelot the First was the third pharaoh of the

00:21:44.801 --> 00:21:47.663
22nd dynasty and the father of Osorkon the Second.

00:21:48.395 --> 00:21:50.357
His reign lasted 14 years,

00:21:50.418 --> 00:21:55.381
between 887 and 873 BC.

00:21:55.681 --> 00:21:58.463
This was a widespread custom in ancient Egypt.

00:21:58.784 --> 00:22:00.585
When a pharaoh ascended to the throne,

00:22:01.184 --> 00:22:03.047
he had to bury his own father,

00:22:03.586 --> 00:22:04.548
his own predecessor.

00:22:05.067 --> 00:22:11.133
So it's not totally illogical to find a vault dedicated to his father inside a king's tomb.

00:22:12.494 --> 00:22:13.274
Unfortunately,

00:22:13.494 --> 00:22:15.336
the sarcophagus is also empty.

00:22:15.804 --> 00:22:19.026
Apart from a few gold fragments left behind by the thieves,

00:22:20.846 --> 00:22:23.407
the second tomb is a big disappointment for Monte,

00:22:23.788 --> 00:22:25.009
and he makes no secret of it.

00:22:25.009 --> 00:22:27.289
It's not going to be like this for the whole campaign.

00:22:27.951 --> 00:22:29.652
Despite this new disappointment,

00:22:29.951 --> 00:22:32.573
the archaeologist continues to explore the tomb.

00:22:35.275 --> 00:22:36.836
He returns to the first tomb,

00:22:37.135 --> 00:22:39.717
which he attributes to the Pharaoh Osorkon II.

00:22:40.597 --> 00:22:44.460
He detects the presence of a large granite block in the masonry of the walls.

00:22:45.816 --> 00:22:47.417
Could this be a new passageway?

00:22:50.817 --> 00:22:55.440
This granite is set into the limestone masonry with mortar joints.

00:22:56.759 --> 00:22:59.341
A small section of this mortar has fallen away,

00:22:59.921 --> 00:23:02.221
revealing a very small opening behind it.

00:23:02.903 --> 00:23:04.503
But there is a void behind it,

00:23:04.942 --> 00:23:06.524
and so they hope.

00:23:06.724 --> 00:23:11.567
That maybe this third chamber is still intact and hasn't been visited by thieves.

00:23:12.848 --> 00:23:13.589
Unfortunately,

00:23:13.929 --> 00:23:18.332
the sarcophagus next to it makes it impossible to extract the heavy obstruction.

00:23:19.173 --> 00:23:24.416
The Egyptologist scratches around to enlarge the opening and makes a wonderful observation.

00:23:25.397 --> 00:23:29.880
It is indeed a vault occupied by a gigantic granite sarcophagus.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:32.542
The obvious finally seems clear.

00:23:33.142 --> 00:23:35.604
It was here in this mysterious chamber.

00:23:35.783 --> 00:23:37.483
As Monte called it at the time,

00:23:37.865 --> 00:23:39.484
that the tomb's main occupant,

00:23:39.625 --> 00:23:40.584
Ozorkon II,

00:23:40.744 --> 00:23:42.245
was probably laid to rest.

00:23:42.965 --> 00:23:45.906
But it would take a little more patience before they could be certain.

00:23:48.246 --> 00:23:53.588
He would quickly realize that he hasn't reached the end of this discovery.

00:23:54.108 --> 00:23:54.728
First of all,

00:23:54.968 --> 00:23:56.909
he has found a royal tomb,

00:23:57.849 --> 00:23:59.289
something nobody has done for many,

00:23:59.349 --> 00:24:00.030
many years.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:05.331
But also looming over everything is the prospect of war.

00:24:05.903 --> 00:24:06.605
Something which,

00:24:06.764 --> 00:24:07.965
if it comes,

00:24:08.705 --> 00:24:10.727
will destroy his plans for the future.

00:24:11.347 --> 00:24:13.328
Despite the frightening danger ahead,

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:18.673
Pierre Monte is determined to see his excavation campaign at Tanis through to the end.

00:24:19.513 --> 00:24:20.974
Before returning to France,

00:24:21.314 --> 00:24:26.117
the Egyptologist decides to continue clearing the roof of the necropolis for a while longer,

00:24:26.678 --> 00:24:29.920
even though he knows he won't be finished before the end of the season.

00:24:30.741 --> 00:24:33.703
It's here that he makes an exciting and promising discovery.

00:24:33.983 --> 00:24:36.865
That will finally fulfil all of his expectations.

00:24:40.808 --> 00:24:42.409
He follows the ceiling,

00:24:42.869 --> 00:24:43.289
the roof,

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:45.731
of this tomb,

00:24:46.511 --> 00:24:52.395
and begins to realise that there is in fact another building immediately next to it.

00:24:54.217 --> 00:25:00.221
He sees that all the roof beams and panels are perfectly joined and show no signs of tampering.

00:25:02.062 --> 00:25:03.463
And that's a very good sign.

00:25:04.824 --> 00:25:07.525
Pierre Monte chooses to have the panels removed.

00:25:08.045 --> 00:25:08.605
Underneath,

00:25:08.746 --> 00:25:10.086
he finds a square pit,

00:25:10.506 --> 00:25:12.487
1.7 meters on each side,

00:25:12.866 --> 00:25:13.767
filled with soil,

00:25:13.967 --> 00:25:15.008
which he has removed.

00:25:15.668 --> 00:25:16.327
The next day,

00:25:16.508 --> 00:25:16.748
March

00:25:17.288 --> 00:25:18.949
16th, it is completely emptied.

00:25:19.349 --> 00:25:20.449
He goes down the shaft,

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:21.490
and for once,

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:22.630
he is not disappointed.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:24.831
Two meters,

00:25:24.951 --> 00:25:25.631
forty below,

00:25:25.791 --> 00:25:26.912
an opening appears,

00:25:26.991 --> 00:25:27.971
blocked by a wall.

00:25:28.395 --> 00:25:30.376
There are no signs of forced entry.

00:25:32.377 --> 00:25:33.278
He's going to be thinking,

00:25:33.759 --> 00:25:34.858
I have another chance,

00:25:34.899 --> 00:25:35.318
perhaps,

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:36.940
at an intact tomb,

00:25:37.259 --> 00:25:38.941
a burial stuffed full of treasure.

00:25:39.381 --> 00:25:41.662
He thought there might be tombs there,

00:25:42.462 --> 00:25:46.704
but he hadn't expected to find royal tombs and intact ones as well.

00:25:47.865 --> 00:25:49.206
Monte rejoices.

00:25:49.865 --> 00:25:51.287
What could be behind that wall?

00:25:57.432 --> 00:25:58.712
The day comes to an end.

00:25:59.273 --> 00:26:01.595
He postpones the opening until the next day,

00:26:06.478 --> 00:26:06.879
Friday,

00:26:06.959 --> 00:26:07.859
March 17th,

00:26:07.999 --> 00:26:08.480
at dawn.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:14.324
Pierre Monte and his team descend to the bottom of the shaft and set about unsealing the wall.

00:26:15.112 --> 00:26:15.932
And this time,

00:26:16.093 --> 00:26:17.433
it's easier to remove.

00:26:18.474 --> 00:26:21.396
It's lime or plaster mortar,

00:26:21.798 --> 00:26:25.360
so it's relatively easy to loosen them and then remove the blocks.

00:26:26.201 --> 00:26:32.185
The archaeologists first enter a small corridor where they must crawl on all fours.

00:26:33.027 --> 00:26:34.427
And there's no doubt about it:

00:26:34.788 --> 00:26:35.388
it's a vault,

00:26:35.908 --> 00:26:37.230
and it's an intact vault.

00:26:39.251 --> 00:26:42.614
The walls are adorned with inscriptions and a royal cartouche,

00:26:42.993 --> 00:26:44.433
that of Susenest the First,

00:26:44.753 --> 00:26:48.355
an important pharaoh of the city of Tanis in the 11th century BC.

00:26:49.856 --> 00:26:50.836
The name on the wall,

00:26:52.097 --> 00:26:52.897
he's thinking,

00:26:53.538 --> 00:26:56.098
is the clue to the owner of this tomb.

00:26:57.299 --> 00:27:01.281
But it it isn't the most striking thing that he sees in this chamber.

00:27:02.142 --> 00:27:05.963
A sight which few other archaeologists.

00:27:07.003 --> 00:27:09.946
Had ever seen or even imagined was before

00:27:10.627 --> 00:27:11.668
Montay's eyes.

00:27:13.129 --> 00:27:14.471
In the middle of this cellar,

00:27:14.871 --> 00:27:17.733
unusual and intriguing reflections stand out.

00:27:18.414 --> 00:27:19.735
He couldn't see very well,

00:27:20.736 --> 00:27:24.340
and yet the light catches something glinting.

00:27:25.341 --> 00:27:26.622
It's something metallic.

00:27:33.412 --> 00:27:33.993
Pierre Montet,

00:27:34.013 --> 00:27:35.074
la voit des éléments.

00:27:35.173 --> 00:27:36.755
Pierre Montet sees silver.

00:27:37.696 --> 00:27:43.380
One of the great characteristics of the Tanis treasure is that there are many silver objects.

00:27:43.801 --> 00:27:44.862
This is silver,

00:27:45.802 --> 00:27:47.124
solid silver.

00:27:47.923 --> 00:27:51.667
Nothing like this is known in Egypt at this point.

00:27:53.731 --> 00:27:59.255
Pierre Monte approaches the silvery reflections that illuminate this two by four meter room,

00:27:59.976 --> 00:28:02.436
and what he sees leaves him speechless.

00:28:04.759 --> 00:28:05.939
On the right-hand side,

00:28:06.179 --> 00:28:07.960
on a base of limestone blocks,

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:10.442
rests a sumptuous silver coffin.

00:28:12.303 --> 00:28:15.904
It's quite rare in that it's shaped like an anthropoid coffin,

00:28:16.384 --> 00:28:17.925
meaning in the shape of a man,

00:28:18.386 --> 00:28:19.567
but with the head of a hawk.

00:28:21.247 --> 00:28:25.153
It's very rare for a king to be depicted like this on his sarcophagus.

00:28:25.712 --> 00:28:27.134
It is unique in the world.

00:28:28.255 --> 00:28:30.237
It's a little cracked,

00:28:30.658 --> 00:28:33.641
but the gold elements inside are still visible.

00:28:35.283 --> 00:28:38.666
Pierre Monte is surrounded with wonders that light up the room.

00:28:39.427 --> 00:28:45.791
The archaeologist also discovers that this silver coffin was accompanied by two mummies in very poor condition.

00:28:46.592 --> 00:28:50.094
The rest of the room is cluttered with countless funerary furnishings.

00:28:52.316 --> 00:28:55.057
We also have shabti's and other grave goods,

00:28:55.398 --> 00:28:56.679
so he is so excited.

00:28:57.038 --> 00:29:00.521
And the inscriptions everywhere keep mentioning Susenyi's.

00:29:01.042 --> 00:29:01.162
So

00:29:01.562 --> 00:29:03.202
Monte crawls out,

00:29:03.604 --> 00:29:05.444
thinking maybe this is Susenyi's.

00:29:05.585 --> 00:29:11.568
Every indication is that nobody has entered this room since the last burial was placed there.

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:13.750
This time,

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:15.231
Pierre Monte is right.

00:29:15.692 --> 00:29:18.534
A month after his discovery at the Tarnis necropolis,

00:29:18.834 --> 00:29:21.276
he has finally unearthed an intact tomb.

00:29:21.875 --> 00:29:23.237
And not just any tomb.

00:29:25.558 --> 00:29:27.661
Nothing like this has ever been found before.

00:29:27.681 --> 00:29:29.122
It's completely unique.

00:29:29.241 --> 00:29:33.385
So he was rewarded beyond his wildest dreams.

00:29:33.466 --> 00:29:36.388
But there was even more still to come that he didn't know.

00:29:37.109 --> 00:29:39.150
End of the day on March seventeenth.

00:29:39.371 --> 00:29:39.971
Incredible.

00:29:45.496 --> 00:29:49.879
A contingent of armed guards is immediately sent to Tanis to secure the area.

00:29:51.142 --> 00:29:52.548
This discovery is,

00:29:52.549 --> 00:29:52.889
of course,

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:53.613
a sensation.

00:29:54.455 --> 00:29:56.856
And news spreads like wildfire.

00:29:57.076 --> 00:30:01.419
An intact Pharaoh's tomb and its fabulous treasure have been discovered.

00:30:02.098 --> 00:30:04.000
This is potentially a new

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:06.701
Tutankhamun for the nineteen thirties.

00:30:07.341 --> 00:30:08.582
Everybody is very excited,

00:30:09.122 --> 00:30:09.382
and

00:30:10.342 --> 00:30:14.344
Monte suddenly finds that he has all the support that he needs,

00:30:14.443 --> 00:30:16.984
and the interest of some very important people,

00:30:17.105 --> 00:30:19.746
including the King of Egypt,

00:30:19.885 --> 00:30:20.606
King Farouk.

00:30:21.807 --> 00:30:24.208
The nineteen-year-old Egyptian king arrives.

00:30:24.128 --> 00:30:26.669
Lives in tennis to witness the opening of the coffin,

00:30:27.309 --> 00:30:31.950
a moment in history that will bring Pierre Monte face to face with a new enigma.

00:30:32.011 --> 00:30:36.572
It's March twentieth,

00:30:36.573 --> 00:30:37.492
nineteen thirty-nine.

00:30:39.373 --> 00:30:44.134
It is with great emotion that Pierre Monte and King Farouk approach the sovereign's coffin,

00:30:44.654 --> 00:30:47.996
not suspecting for a moment that a major surprise awaits them.

00:30:49.759 --> 00:30:51.923
He is going to be thinking that this is

00:30:52.682 --> 00:30:53.484
Susenyan the First.

00:30:53.485 --> 00:30:54.445
The name is on the wall.

00:30:54.465 --> 00:30:55.727
This must surely be

00:30:56.346 --> 00:30:59.490
Susenyan, and yet it's not.

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:07.659
The archaeologists clean the tops of the coffin and realize that the inscriptions show that it is not Susenyan the First buried there.

00:31:10.504 --> 00:31:13.166
But a ruler who is once again called

00:31:14.748 --> 00:31:16.028
Shishank, or Shishank,

00:31:16.249 --> 00:31:16.930
or Shishak,

00:31:16.931 --> 00:31:18.691
the manner in which we call him,

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:21.272
a Shishank who has never been heard of before,

00:31:21.532 --> 00:31:23.173
another kind of mystery.

00:31:23.433 --> 00:31:25.434
And something totally unexpected.

00:31:26.556 --> 00:31:27.797
Who is this Sheshonk?

00:31:28.538 --> 00:31:32.880
Could this be the Sheshonk whose ushetis was found when the necropolis was discovered?

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:36.002
When it turns out not to be Susenese,

00:31:36.823 --> 00:31:38.984
the press might like it that it's Sheshonk.

00:31:40.085 --> 00:31:44.428
Journalists believe that the Sheshonk found in the silver sarcophagus is well known.

00:31:45.068 --> 00:31:45.568
In fact,

00:31:45.769 --> 00:31:49.070
he was one of the most important and famous pharaohs in history.

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:53.684
They are already making this association between Shishank,

00:31:54.525 --> 00:31:56.448
whose body they hope is inside the coffin,

00:31:56.988 --> 00:31:57.989
and Shishak of the Bible.

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:04.437
Shishank in the Old Testament is said to have conquered the city of Jerusalem on the death of the famous King Solomon,

00:32:04.958 --> 00:32:07.281
looting his city and taking everything with him,

00:32:07.601 --> 00:32:09.682
including the legendary Ark of the Covenant.

00:32:10.223 --> 00:32:17.086
The press goes wild over this discovery and begins to believe that the treasures of King Solomon of Jerusalem had finally been found.

00:32:17.346 --> 00:32:27.190
This is the thought that is conveyed to the public that this is the sarcophagus and perhaps inside the body of this important figure from the Bible.

00:32:27.509 --> 00:32:30.270
And so this is how it appears in the newspapers.

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:32.551
Though obviously this is not the case at all.

00:32:33.971 --> 00:32:34.532
For Monte,

00:32:34.832 --> 00:32:36.273
there has never been any doubt.

00:32:36.753 --> 00:32:40.815
The Shishank he has just discovered is absolutely not the one in the Bible.

00:32:41.775 --> 00:32:47.578
Time and thorough archaeological research will identify this silver pharaoh as Shishank II.

00:32:48.138 --> 00:32:50.379
Nothing to do with the plunderer of Jerusalem.

00:32:51.219 --> 00:32:51.940
On the other hand,

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:56.602
the scholar clearly understands that this Shishank is a completely unknown ruler.

00:32:59.202 --> 00:32:59.783
In the tomb,

00:33:00.123 --> 00:33:01.564
in the presence of King Farouk.

00:33:02.003 --> 00:33:04.706
Pierre Monte lifts the lid of the silver coffin.

00:33:05.606 --> 00:33:09.049
One of the most beautiful jewels in ancient Egypt appears.

00:33:10.531 --> 00:33:11.311
There is a mask,

00:33:11.832 --> 00:33:16.236
a gold mask that is almost as beautiful as the mask of Tutankhamun.

00:33:16.736 --> 00:33:19.138
Something of extraordinary beauty.

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:21.201
It's really quite spectacular.

00:33:21.701 --> 00:33:23.502
And then a whole set of jewels,

00:33:23.622 --> 00:33:24.083
bracelets,

00:33:24.144 --> 00:33:25.204
and chest decorations,

00:33:25.444 --> 00:33:27.866
each one finer and more magnificent than the last.

00:33:29.111 --> 00:33:32.235
The two men contemplate the contents of the silver vessel.

00:33:32.817 --> 00:33:36.581
Adornments and jewels are clearly visible and appear to be in place,

00:33:36.882 --> 00:33:37.943
but are tangled together,

00:33:38.043 --> 00:33:41.269
revealing that the burial site has sustained considerable damage.

00:33:41.869 --> 00:33:43.230
The mummy is gone.

00:33:43.791 --> 00:33:45.173
Only the bones remain.

00:33:45.792 --> 00:33:48.335
Unlike the dry climate of the Valley of the Kings,

00:33:48.674 --> 00:33:52.337
the humidity of the Nile Delta is not conducive to preservation.

00:33:52.698 --> 00:34:01.763
The remains of Shoshenk and indeed of the other kings who were found in the cemetery were all reduced to badly decayed skeletons.

00:34:03.045 --> 00:34:04.165
In spite of everything,

00:34:04.506 --> 00:34:06.366
the discovery is extraordinary,

00:34:06.706 --> 00:34:08.168
and the joy is tremendous.

00:34:08.568 --> 00:34:09.889
As for the other two mummies,

00:34:10.289 --> 00:34:12.391
they cannot be immediately identified.

00:34:12.811 --> 00:34:13.351
It is only.

00:34:13.492 --> 00:34:14.052
Much later,

00:34:14.172 --> 00:34:19.276
that the study of a few ushetis found nearby allows us to restore their identity.

00:34:19.695 --> 00:34:21.497
The first is Pharaoh Siyamun,

00:34:21.998 --> 00:34:25.079
one of the successors to Susenes the First during the

00:34:25.559 --> 00:34:26.120
21st Dynasty.

00:34:26.581 --> 00:34:30.222
The second is his successor and the last ruler of the dynasty,

00:34:30.623 --> 00:34:31.704
Susenes the Second.

00:34:32.204 --> 00:34:32.605
Mummies,

00:34:33.245 --> 00:34:33.986
without a coffin,

00:34:34.026 --> 00:34:35.046
without a sarcophagus,

00:34:35.547 --> 00:34:36.567
what does this mean?

00:34:37.663 --> 00:34:42.807
These corpses were probably removed from their original burial sites following ancient looting.

00:34:43.107 --> 00:34:45.449
This vault must surely be a hiding place.

00:34:45.889 --> 00:34:48.750
Pierre Monte continues his exploration of the tomb.

00:34:49.411 --> 00:34:52.493
It became clear to Monte that this was not a tomb built.

00:34:52.413 --> 00:34:54.796
Built for any of those three pharaohs,

00:34:55.256 --> 00:35:00.603
because on the walls of the chamber were inscriptions of Sesostris the First,

00:35:01.344 --> 00:35:02.665
perhaps the greatest of the

00:35:03.266 --> 00:35:04.387
21st Dynasty pharaohs.

00:35:04.668 --> 00:35:06.450
Where is his burial chamber?

00:35:07.632 --> 00:35:09.833
As he clears away these precious treasures,

00:35:10.233 --> 00:35:13.954
Pierre Monte takes a closer look at the walls of the burial chamber.

00:35:14.394 --> 00:35:17.974
He's now had experience of how these tombs work,

00:35:18.414 --> 00:35:21.816
and he realizes there are places which are plugs,

00:35:21.856 --> 00:35:25.497
and places where you can maybe enter other chambers.

00:35:27.378 --> 00:35:31.039
Concealed behind this decor are two square openings.

00:35:31.579 --> 00:35:34.760
Could these well-sealed passages lead to new vaults?

00:35:39.368 --> 00:35:41.489
But there's not enough time to go any further.

00:35:42.310 --> 00:35:44.591
The end of the campaign is fast approaching,

00:35:44.891 --> 00:35:48.233
and there's already a lot to do to take care of what's been discovered.

00:35:49.114 --> 00:35:50.134
Further exploration,

00:35:50.414 --> 00:35:51.214
full of promise,

00:35:51.556 --> 00:35:54.076
was wisely postponed until the following year.

00:35:55.016 --> 00:35:56.677
Before returning to France,

00:35:57.097 --> 00:36:01.280
Pierre Monte longs to discover the secrets of the mysterious chamber,

00:36:01.681 --> 00:36:03.641
for the giant sarcophagus seen in.

00:36:03.581 --> 00:36:04.342
In the other tomb,

00:36:05.443 --> 00:36:06.745
around the granite plug,

00:36:07.045 --> 00:36:08.307
which cannot be removed,

00:36:08.567 --> 00:36:09.909
he enlarges the opening,

00:36:10.129 --> 00:36:14.895
which three weeks earlier had provided a brief glimpse of the vault's interior.

00:36:16.036 --> 00:36:18.559
Piermonte manages to get a slightly

00:36:18.739 --> 00:36:19.601
Larger opening,

00:36:20.322 --> 00:36:22.125
about thirty centimeters wide,

00:36:22.885 --> 00:36:24.868
but it's still a very narrow opening.

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:27.532
He tries to enter the tomb,

00:36:28.013 --> 00:36:28.775
to no avail.

00:36:29.755 --> 00:36:30.837
On March twenty-fourth,

00:36:30.998 --> 00:36:33.240
a very slim worker tries once again,

00:36:33.721 --> 00:36:34.742
but is unsuccessful.

00:36:37.144 --> 00:36:37.744
In the end,

00:36:37.985 --> 00:36:43.505
it's a relatively slender teenager who makes his way through the narrow opening and into the room.

00:36:44.827 --> 00:36:47.947
And it is that member of the team that is able to say to Monte,

00:36:48.106 --> 00:36:48.367
"Yes,

00:36:48.608 --> 00:36:50.028
there is something inside here."

00:36:50.588 --> 00:36:55.269
The young teenager circles the giant sarcophagus and carefully inspects the vault.

00:36:55.668 --> 00:36:57.829
There is obviously an opening that's been sealed,

00:36:57.990 --> 00:36:59.891
so most certainly another thief's hole,

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:03.412
which provides hope that they will ultimately be able to penetrate the vault.

00:37:03.751 --> 00:37:05.192
Pierre Monte then places.

00:37:05.391 --> 00:37:07.393
All his available workers in this area,

00:37:07.793 --> 00:37:11.735
and asks them to speed up the opening of the roof since time is running out.

00:37:12.677 --> 00:37:13.856
The end of the campaign,

00:37:14.217 --> 00:37:15.918
scheduled for the first week of April,

00:37:16.119 --> 00:37:17.159
is fast approaching.

00:37:20.319 --> 00:37:20.699
On March

00:37:21.359 --> 00:37:28.182
26th, the Egyptologist finally enters the Great Vault and discovers a granite sarcophagus of colossal dimensions,

00:37:28.442 --> 00:37:30.443
over three and a half meters long.

00:37:30.723 --> 00:37:33.784
Is this indeed the sarcophagus of Osorkon II,

00:37:34.164 --> 00:37:37.905
as all the inscriptions in the previously discovered rooms suggest?

00:37:38.166 --> 00:37:39.946
There's nothing yet to confirm this,

00:37:40.386 --> 00:37:41.447
but there's little doubt.

00:37:42.248 --> 00:37:45.289
The chamber appears to have been pierced by ancient looters,

00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:48.512
which does not bode well for the condition of its contents.

00:37:48.932 --> 00:37:52.514
Only three mummies in very poor condition are found inside.

00:37:52.535 --> 00:37:53.394
The following year,

00:37:53.796 --> 00:37:58.077
there are debris in the sarcophagus from a royal coffin,

00:37:59.079 --> 00:38:00.320
but whether these mummies,

00:38:00.659 --> 00:38:01.079
either of them,

00:38:01.099 --> 00:38:04.561
are his or they are of later intrusive burials,

00:38:05.063 --> 00:38:05.702
is unknown.

00:38:07.768 --> 00:38:11.170
But while clearing away the rubble that had fallen from the thief's hole,

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:14.372
the archaeologists make an unexpected discovery.

00:38:15.311 --> 00:38:21.735
At the head of the large sarcophagus is the beautifully sculpted quartzite lid of a much smaller sarcophagus.

00:38:23.983 --> 00:38:25.604
Second sarcophagus,

00:38:26.245 --> 00:38:31.828
which is inscribed and proves to belong to a son of Azorcon II,

00:38:32.189 --> 00:38:33.890
who apparently died in childhood.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:36.692
This is a prince by the name of Hornact.

00:38:37.492 --> 00:38:38.573
We knew almost.

00:38:38.813 --> 00:38:39.174
Nothing,

00:38:39.194 --> 00:38:40.235
if anything at all,

00:38:40.395 --> 00:38:41.737
about this individual.

00:38:42.097 --> 00:38:45.602
The results of the 1939 campaign are already considerable:

00:38:45.942 --> 00:38:47.223
three new royal tombs,

00:38:47.504 --> 00:38:53.531
one of which is intact; five stone sarcophagi; one silver coffin; a gold mask; an extraordinary few.

00:38:53.492 --> 00:38:57.275
Funerary furnishings and promises of more discoveries to come.

00:38:57.655 --> 00:39:02.479
The Egyptology community and the press are enthusiastic about this extraordinary achievement.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:08.443
Pierre Monte is uncovering a whole swath of Egyptian history from this

00:39:09.125 --> 00:39:10.626
21st and 22nd dynasty,

00:39:11.166 --> 00:39:13.548
of which very little was known at the time.

00:39:14.128 --> 00:39:14.969
But poor man,

00:39:15.068 --> 00:39:17.091
he had something quite serious to deal with,

00:39:17.591 --> 00:39:19.992
because there were huge rumblings in Europe,

00:39:20.354 --> 00:39:23.195
because this was the start of the Second World War.

00:39:23.596 --> 00:39:27.461
And this was going to overshadow his life and his discovery.

00:39:33.629 --> 00:39:35.711
The end of 1939,

00:39:35.711 --> 00:39:40.076
France and Germany had been officially at war for four months without direct combat.

00:39:40.757 --> 00:39:42.740
Hitler is busy destroying Poland.

00:39:43.464 --> 00:39:45.926
Pierre Monte teaches in Clermont-Ferrand,

00:39:46.206 --> 00:39:49.809
where the University of Strasbourg withdrew at the start of the conflict.

00:39:49.969 --> 00:39:51.510
This is a very dramatic time.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.131
What does the future hold?

00:39:53.671 --> 00:39:59.195
It's a complicated time for Pierre Monte because he's not sure he'll be able to return to Tunis.

00:40:00.035 --> 00:40:01.436
Despite international tension,

00:40:01.776 --> 00:40:03.938
the archaeologist wants to return to Egypt,

00:40:04.118 --> 00:40:06.259
where the treasure of Sesostris the First awaits him.

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:08.900
Though France is in a state of war,

00:40:09.221 --> 00:40:13.043
he somehow manages to find funding for a new excavation campaign.

00:40:13.884 --> 00:40:17.746
Although he imagines finding new jewels in the royal necropolis of Tanis,

00:40:18.168 --> 00:40:22.309
he doesn't expect to make a discovery as important as that of Tutankhamun's tomb.

00:40:24.471 --> 00:40:25.612
It's January 15,

00:40:25.614 --> 00:40:26.612
1940.

00:40:27.373 --> 00:40:29.154
Pierre Monte is back in Tanis.

00:40:29.855 --> 00:40:35.378
He can't wait to find out what hides behind the wall of the chamber where Shoshenk II lay.

00:40:37.367 --> 00:40:42.811
Pierre Monte immediately sets to work on the potential openings in the walls of Sheshonk II's burial chamber.

00:40:43.351 --> 00:40:47.054
They are covered in decorations bearing the image and cartouches of two pharaohs:

00:40:47.394 --> 00:40:50.036
Susena's the first and his successor Amenemope.

00:40:50.757 --> 00:40:51.617
Without hesitation,

00:40:51.878 --> 00:40:52.257
the Egypt.

00:40:52.177 --> 00:40:55.320
Egyptologist chooses to begin by exploring the vault of the first.

00:40:55.640 --> 00:41:05.188
The problem at the outset is that the doorway is blocked by an enormous granite blocking stone,

00:41:05.688 --> 00:41:06.829
enormously heavy,

00:41:07.289 --> 00:41:13.074
and the big question is how does one actually move this huge lump of granite out of the way?

00:41:14.055 --> 00:41:17.978
It must be possible because they put it in to start with.

00:41:19.244 --> 00:41:23.689
They gently trim away the contours of this two-meter-long block on a slight slope.

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:28.934
It's a real challenge for the archaeologists to free this enormous stone,

00:41:29.294 --> 00:41:33.057
which is in fact part of an obelisk put in place by the ancient Egyptians.

00:41:34.940 --> 00:41:37.763
Pierre Monte and his team try a clever technique.

00:41:40.255 --> 00:41:44.318
He manages to pass a steel cable,

00:41:45.018 --> 00:41:49.902
which enables him to pass large marine ropes several times around the block.

00:41:51.403 --> 00:41:55.005
A set of ropes is attached to a hoist installed in the anti.

00:41:55.025 --> 00:41:59.871
The block is pulled very gradually in an attempt to extract it.

00:42:01.293 --> 00:42:02.975
It's laborious and very slow.

00:42:03.375 --> 00:42:08.081
It takes hours and hours to finally move the block a few centimeters.

00:42:08.381 --> 00:42:09.322
But after a few days,

00:42:09.683 --> 00:42:10.003
suddenly.

00:42:10.404 --> 00:42:11.306
Everything changes,

00:42:11.925 --> 00:42:14.449
and the obelisk is moving much easier.

00:42:15.069 --> 00:42:16.992
It seems it's rolling on something.

00:42:17.793 --> 00:42:25.242
What they don't yet know is that they're being helped by bronze rollers placed there when this piece of obelisk was laid three millennia ago.

00:42:26.332 --> 00:42:27.773
This ingenious technique,

00:42:27.993 --> 00:42:29.394
well known in ancient Egypt,

00:42:29.735 --> 00:42:32.977
was to prove an invaluable tool for the tombs explorers.

00:42:33.336 --> 00:42:34.418
And once they realize this,

00:42:34.678 --> 00:42:36.999
they simply have to reverse the process,

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:40.782
and now exactly those same rollers are used by Monte and his team.

00:42:41.061 --> 00:42:42.244
To open it again,

00:42:42.623 --> 00:42:43.505
and finally,

00:42:44.605 --> 00:42:46.027
it moves out of its position,

00:42:46.628 --> 00:42:50.012
allowing proper access to the chamber for the first time.

00:42:50.574 --> 00:42:51.153
And then,

00:42:51.375 --> 00:42:51.994
at last,

00:42:52.295 --> 00:42:53.257
the great discovery.

00:42:53.576 --> 00:42:55.759
It is February 6.

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:56.360
Sixteenth,

00:42:56.461 --> 00:42:57.300
nineteen forty,

00:42:58.041 --> 00:43:00.324
two days after starting to remove the stone,

00:43:00.684 --> 00:43:06.347
Pierre Monte enters a narrow room measuring five meters long by one point seven meters wide.

00:43:06.769 --> 00:43:09.291
A magnificent vision appears before him:

00:43:09.791 --> 00:43:14.135
a pink granite sarcophagus surrounded by the most wondrous treasures.

00:43:14.454 --> 00:43:16.476
And they are confronted by an incredible sight:

00:43:16.557 --> 00:43:21.280
a monumental stone sarcophagus surrounded by burial equipment.

00:43:21.641 --> 00:43:23.561
This is a very very exciting moment.

00:43:24.383 --> 00:43:25.704
It's the start of something.

00:43:26.164 --> 00:43:26.785
Totally new.

00:43:27.045 --> 00:43:28.125
It's probably like

00:43:29.186 --> 00:43:31.227
Neil Armstrong setting foot on the moon.

00:43:32.907 --> 00:43:40.231
It opens a whole new chapter for Egyptology and for ancient Egyptian history writing.

00:43:40.431 --> 00:43:41.552
Ancient Egyptian history.

00:43:42.679 --> 00:43:48.567
The French Egyptologist sees hundreds of Ushabti and an incredible collection of precious tableware,

00:43:48.987 --> 00:43:50.969
masterpieces in gold and silver.

00:43:51.750 --> 00:43:56.335
Most of these objects bear inscriptions revealing the insignia of Sesostris I.

00:43:56.835 --> 00:43:58.237
There is no doubt about it.

00:43:58.757 --> 00:43:59.837
This is his vault,

00:44:00.257 --> 00:44:02.259
and its contents are intact.

00:44:02.838 --> 00:44:05.219
The tomb has been undisturbed.

00:44:05.980 --> 00:44:07.781
No one has been in there before.

00:44:08.181 --> 00:44:13.424
They start finding all these wonderful objects that you would expect in a royal tomb.

00:44:13.644 --> 00:44:13.764
So,

00:44:14.884 --> 00:44:17.646
probably the most spectacular discovery in Egypt since

00:44:17.987 --> 00:44:18.666
Tutankhamun.

00:44:20.135 --> 00:44:20.816
On February

00:44:21.317 --> 00:44:23.918
20th, Pierre Monte opens the sarcophagus.

00:44:24.577 --> 00:44:25.038
The lid,

00:44:25.157 --> 00:44:26.699
weighing 800 kilos,

00:44:26.838 --> 00:44:28.700
is gently lifted using levers.

00:44:29.621 --> 00:44:30.240
Inside,

00:44:30.561 --> 00:44:33.322
two other coffins are assembled like Russian dolls.

00:44:34.943 --> 00:44:36.003
As with Tutankhamun,

00:44:36.344 --> 00:44:39.105
we also have this system of nesting dolls,

00:44:39.605 --> 00:44:41.586
meaning a coffin inside,

00:44:41.786 --> 00:44:43.427
which is a coffin inside,

00:44:43.626 --> 00:44:44.488
which is a coffin.

00:44:45.847 --> 00:44:52.353
The last of these is one of the most beautiful jewels in the history of archaeology—a new silver coffin,

00:44:52.454 --> 00:44:54.076
unlike anything in the world.

00:44:54.916 --> 00:44:55.476
This time,

00:44:55.717 --> 00:44:59.280
it doesn't depict a falcon's head like the one on Shoshenk II.

00:45:00.641 --> 00:45:02.842
This is the face of the pharaoh.

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:13.083
This coffin is extremely fine,

00:45:13.483 --> 00:45:14.085
refined,

00:45:14.545 --> 00:45:15.326
sculpted.

00:45:15.365 --> 00:45:16.547
It's a pure marvel,

00:45:16.807 --> 00:45:17.648
a pure jewel.

00:45:17.668 --> 00:45:17.788
So,

00:45:18.308 --> 00:45:19.911
this was something,

00:45:20.592 --> 00:45:21.612
excepted silver,

00:45:22.094 --> 00:45:26.858
to rival what we had in Tutankhamun's most inner coffin.

00:45:28.056 --> 00:45:29.619
When the silver lid is lifted,

00:45:29.940 --> 00:45:31.483
a new surprise awaits.

00:45:32.104 --> 00:45:32.925
And then inside that,

00:45:32.925 --> 00:45:33.286
of course,

00:45:33.306 --> 00:45:36.392
is a spectacular gold mask and the mummy of the king,

00:45:36.773 --> 00:45:39.557
covered with jewels and spectacular gold.

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:44.800
Unfortunately,

00:45:45.121 --> 00:45:47.041
the mummy is in very poor condition,

00:45:47.481 --> 00:45:52.246
but it is surrounded by marvelous jewels such as these chest plates set with fine stones,

00:45:52.646 --> 00:45:54.086
cases for the pharaoh's fingers,

00:45:54.447 --> 00:45:55.108
these rings,

00:45:55.208 --> 00:45:56.048
and these bands.

00:45:57.568 --> 00:45:58.849
There are no fewer.

00:45:58.769 --> 00:46:02.291
Fewer than twenty-six bracelets and numerous necklaces,

00:46:02.692 --> 00:46:03.331
very heavy.

00:46:04.252 --> 00:46:06.614
One necklace alone weighs eight kilos.

00:46:07.273 --> 00:46:07.793
A second,

00:46:08.154 --> 00:46:08.914
almost as much,

00:46:09.454 --> 00:46:10.335
seven kilos.

00:46:11.195 --> 00:46:14.938
A bracelet weighs almost two kilos of solid gold.

00:46:17.598 --> 00:46:20.619
It's a colossal discovery for Egyptian archaeology,

00:46:21.181 --> 00:46:23.201
a treasure worthy of Tutankhamen,

00:46:23.721 --> 00:46:27.364
and the discovery of a powerful pharaoh from the twenty-first dynasty.

00:46:29.315 --> 00:46:33.597
Suenos the First is not just any pharaoh in Tanis,

00:46:33.958 --> 00:46:35.579
but the king who built Tanis.

00:46:36.038 --> 00:46:40.221
He built the great surrounding wall that encloses the city's major temple,

00:46:40.621 --> 00:46:41.780
the Temple of Amun,

00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:45.063
as well as a number of other buildings that bear his name.

00:46:46.963 --> 00:46:49.523
His tomb expands the field of knowledge of the

00:46:50.065 --> 00:46:51.204
21st Pharaonic dynasty.

00:46:52.065 --> 00:46:54.565
Despite the exhumation of this precious treasure,

00:46:54.947 --> 00:46:57.728
the Egyptologist is not at the end of his surprises.

00:46:59.623 --> 00:47:00.684
On April sixteenth,

00:47:00.784 --> 00:47:01.605
nineteen forty,

00:47:02.025 --> 00:47:07.951
two months to the day after the great discovery of the silver sarcophagus and treasures of Susanna's the first,

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:13.434
Pierre Monte inspects the second burial chamber behind the other blocking stone.

00:47:13.634 --> 00:47:15.335
And so he opens that up,

00:47:15.436 --> 00:47:19.659
and then again finds another largely intact tomb.

00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:21.641
That of Amenemope,

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:22.421
the son of

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:24.521
Suseinis, the first.

00:47:24.882 --> 00:47:25.403
Once more,

00:47:26.143 --> 00:47:29.405
a chamber full of wonderful things,

00:47:29.545 --> 00:47:30.425
full of treasures.

00:47:31.425 --> 00:47:32.046
Once more,

00:47:32.365 --> 00:47:34.887
with the mummy that is almost,

00:47:35.188 --> 00:47:36.949
that has fallen into dust.

00:47:37.809 --> 00:47:38.809
But once more,

00:47:39.170 --> 00:47:41.391
he finds another king in Tanis.

00:47:42.291 --> 00:47:44.773
Such an event should have shaken the whole world,

00:47:45.432 --> 00:47:47.535
but it is the spring of 1940,

00:47:48.014 --> 00:47:50.256
and France has just suffered a heavy defeat.

00:47:50.155 --> 00:47:51.836
Defeat at the hands of the Nazis.

00:47:52.396 --> 00:47:53.737
Paris is occupied,

00:47:54.098 --> 00:47:55.940
as is the northern half of the country.

00:47:56.359 --> 00:47:58.701
It doesn't make the news media the way

00:47:59.442 --> 00:48:00.341
Tarenkamen did,

00:48:00.382 --> 00:48:01.762
or the way you would think it would.

00:48:01.942 --> 00:48:05.326
The discovery was found just at the wrong time,

00:48:06.706 --> 00:48:07.527
and as a result,

00:48:07.567 --> 00:48:10.608
it never got the publicity it deserved at the time,

00:48:11.128 --> 00:48:11.589
and really,

00:48:11.750 --> 00:48:12.509
subsequently,

00:48:12.649 --> 00:48:14.370
seems to have been lost in obscurity.

00:48:16.172 --> 00:48:17.052
Back in France,

00:48:17.353 --> 00:48:20.795
Pierre Monte tries to survive in this chaotic territory.

00:48:21.635 --> 00:48:25.198
Egypt and the Pharaohs would not really be mentioned for years to come.

00:48:25.759 --> 00:48:28.740
And when he is able to return in 1945,

00:48:28.981 --> 00:48:30.722
as the war draws to a close,

00:48:31.122 --> 00:48:32.903
he learns some terrible news.

00:48:35.861 --> 00:48:38.163
The site was looted in 1943,

00:48:38.603 --> 00:48:40.144
and some of Tanis's treasures,

00:48:40.424 --> 00:48:43.087
which had been safely stored in the Cairo Museum,

00:48:43.507 --> 00:48:44.728
have also disappeared.

00:48:45.549 --> 00:48:48.231
Is a horrible thing for any excavator.

00:48:49.032 --> 00:48:50.133
Some were found later,

00:48:50.513 --> 00:48:52.335
but others have never been found,

00:48:52.835 --> 00:48:54.977
and that's a loss for science.

00:48:55.938 --> 00:48:56.538
Dejected,

00:48:56.919 --> 00:48:59.581
Monte nevertheless resumes his excavations,

00:48:59.981 --> 00:49:00.982
and the following year,

00:49:01.002 --> 00:49:03.024
a stunning new discovery awaits him.

00:49:03.980 --> 00:49:04.920
It is now spring,

00:49:04.922 --> 00:49:06.161
1946.

00:49:06.942 --> 00:49:09.523
After all his unique and dazzling discoveries,

00:49:09.923 --> 00:49:13.206
Pierre Monte unearths a new intact burial site,

00:49:13.686 --> 00:49:15.168
somewhat different from the others.

00:49:16.107 --> 00:49:17.768
It's not a king,

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:18.529
but a high

00:49:18.449 --> 00:49:22.552
High-ranking military general called Wenjabaou Injed,

00:49:23.253 --> 00:49:28.337
and he was a favoured soldier at the court of Susenyos I,

00:49:28.637 --> 00:49:32.680
and had clearly been given a very lavish burial by the king.

00:49:33.561 --> 00:49:36.103
His vault contains sumptuous gold objects,

00:49:36.363 --> 00:49:41.447
including this stunningly realistic mask and jewels as magnificent as they are refined.

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:43.141
In the end,

00:49:43.481 --> 00:49:50.265
Pierre Monte unearthed an exceptional funerary complex designed for at least thirteen royal and princely burials,

00:49:50.766 --> 00:49:53.086
six of which were intact or nearly so.

00:49:53.768 --> 00:49:56.389
More than half a century after Pierre Monte's death,

00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:57.230
the capital.

00:49:57.309 --> 00:50:00.373
Of Susenyan I still fascinates archaeologists.

00:50:00.893 --> 00:50:05.039
The French Tannis excavation mission continues to carry out research there,

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:07.181
and now uses modern technology.

00:50:08.103 --> 00:50:11.907
Let's not forget that the site covers an area of 220 hectares.

00:50:12.248 --> 00:50:17.610
And that the temples that have been excavated represent only about 15 percent of the entire site.

00:50:17.891 --> 00:50:18.871
The city itself,

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:20.271
which surrounds the temples,

00:50:20.532 --> 00:50:21.853
has never been explored.

00:50:21.854 --> 00:50:23.834
The whole city is there,

00:50:23.974 --> 00:50:25.014
lying under the ground.

00:50:25.054 --> 00:50:28.396
So there's hundreds of years of work left to do at Tanis.

00:50:28.476 --> 00:50:30.037
So it's it will yield,

00:50:30.157 --> 00:50:30.517
I'm sure,

00:50:30.597 --> 00:50:35.099
more spectacular discoveries as the the days and and years go by.

00:50:36.440 --> 00:50:39.481
A site that may have slipped under the radar due to history,

00:50:39.902 --> 00:50:43.123
but which could well be the focus of attention in the years to come.

00:50:43.963 --> 00:50:52.067
Who knows if fabulous new treasures will be discovered by a new generation of adventurers in search of lost pharaohs?

